Generative AI / LLM Fundamentals
1. What is an LLM, and how does it work at a high level?
Ans: An LLM, or Large Language Model, is a neural network trained on a massive amount of text to understand and generate human-like language.
At a high level, it converts text into tokens, processes them using Transformer architecture and self-attention to understand context, and predicts the next most likely token.
2. Explain the Transformer architecture.
Ans: Transformer is a neural network architecture introduced for processing sequential data
  मान लो input है:
  “Rahul is going to school.”
  Transformer इसे roughly इन steps में process करता है:

  Tokenization
    पूरे sentence को छोटे-छोटे tokens में तोड़ा जाता है।
    जैसे: Rahul | is | going | to | school
  Embedding
    हर token को एक numeric vector में बदल दिया जाता है, ताकि model उसे mathematically process कर सके।
  Positional Information
    Transformer को यह बताना जरूरी है कि कौन-सा word पहले और कौन-सा बाद में आया है। इसलिए हर token के साथ उसकी position की information जोड़ी जाती है।
  Self-Attention ⭐
    यह Transformer का सबसे important part है।
    Model हर word को बाकी words के साथ compare करके देखता है कि कौन-से words एक-दूसरे से ज्यादा related हैं।
    उदाहरण:
      “The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired.”
      
      यहाँ “it” किसके लिए है?
      Self-attention model को context देखकर समझने में मदद करता है कि “it” → animal को refer कर रहा है।

  Multi-Head Attention
    Model एक ही समय में अलग-अलग प्रकार के relationships देखता है—जैसे grammar, meaning, और word relationships।
  Feed-Forward Network
    Attention से मिली information को आगे process करके model और useful representation बनाता है।
  यह process कई layers में repeat होता है
    Transformer में ऐसे कई layers होते हैं। हर layer input की understanding को और बेहतर बनाती है।
  Output / Prediction
    GPT जैसे decoder-only models में अंत में model अगला सबसे संभावित token predict करता है और इसी तरह एक-एक करके पूरा answer generate करता है।
  एक लाइन में याद रखो:
    Text → Tokens → Embeddings → Position → Self-Attention → Feed Forward → Multiple Layers → Next Token Prediction

3. What is self-attention?
Ans: Self-attention is a mechanism in the Transformer 
    Self-attention allows each token to look at other tokens and assign importance to them, helping the Transformer understand the context and relationships within the input.”
    It calculates relationships between tokens so that the model can focus more on the relevant words when understanding the context.
    How does it work?
      Each token is converted into three vectors:
      Query (Q) → मैं किस information को ढूंढ रहा हूँ?
      Key (K) → मेरे पास कौन-सी information है?
      Value (V) → actual information जो आगे pass होगी
4. Type of Transformers?
Ans: Encoder-only → Understand      ex: BERT      usecase: Classification
    Decoder-only → Generate        ex: GPT/Llama, gemini.      usecase: Chatbots, text generation, coding
    Encoder-Decoder → Understand → Generate      ex: T5/BART    usecase: Translation, summarization

5. Why are Transformers better than RNNs for many NLP tasks?
Ans: RNN stands for Recurrent Neural Network. It is a neural network designed to process sequential data,
where the output from the previous step is used as input to the next step. This allows the model to maintain a kind of memory of previous information.”
Transformers are generally better than RNNs for many NLP tasks because they can process tokens in parallel, while RNNs process them sequentially.
Transformers also use self-attention, which allows them to capture relationships between distant words more effectively.

6. What is tokenization? Why does it matter?
Ans: Tokenization is the process of breaking text into smaller units called tokens that an LLM can process and understand.
  Word-level tokenization
  Character-level tokenization
  Subword tokenization  -  unhappiness → [un, happiness]

7. What are temperature, top-k, and top-p?
Ans: Temperature controls how sharp or random the probability distribution is.
      Low temperature = predictable
      High temperature = creative/random
    Example in RAG:
      In RAG / factual application: put temp low because we want consistent and factual answers.
      if we want creative writing than we have to put High temprature.
  Top-K limits the model to the K most probable tokens.
  Top-P is also called Nucleus Sampling.
  this is different, instead of taking exact 5 or 10 candidate/token. Top P says Consider enough of the most probable tokens until their cumulative probability reaches P.
  For each token we have probability and cumulative. and suppose top_P = 90 is setted 
  than Model will select the smallest group of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches approximately 90%.
  ex: from top-k token, probability value will be sum up to reach 90.

  Very important: Temperature doesn't mean "accuracy"
  Lower temperature makes generation more deterministic and focused, but it doesn't inherently make the model more factually accurate.”

  These are decoding or sampling parameters that control how an LLM selects the next token. Temperature controls the randomness of the probability distribution—lower 
  temperature makes the output more deterministic, while higher temperature increases diversity. 
  Top-K restricts sampling to the K most probable tokens. 
  Top-P, or nucleus sampling, dynamically selects the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches the specified threshold. 
  

8. What is the context window?
Ans: Context window is the maximum number of tokens an LLM can process at one time,
    including the input prompt, conversation history, retrieved documents, and sometimes the generated output.
    Suppose a model has a 128K token context window
    128K tokens = Input + Conversation + RAG Context + Output

9. What happens when the prompt exceeds the context window?
Ans: When the prompt exceeds the model's context window, the request cannot fit within the model's maximum token limit.
    it may return a context-length error, or the application may truncate some of the input.
    leading to incomplete or incorrect answers

    How do we solve it?
    In a RAG system, common solutions are:
      Reduce Top-K → retrieve fewer chunks.
      Reranking → keep only the most relevant chunks.
      Chunk-size optimization → use appropriately sized chunks.
      Context compression → remove redundant/irrelevant information.
      Summarization → summarize older conversation or documents.
      Conversation management → keep recent/important messages and drop less useful history.
      Use a model with a larger context window when appropriate.
        Not everytime solution is to chnage model with large context window. 
        Firstly, optimize retrieval and context construction so that the LLM receives the smallest amount of highly relevant information needed to answer the query.”

10. What is hallucination, and why does it happen?
Ans: Hallucination is when an LLM generates information that sounds confident and plausible 
    but is factually incorrect, unsupported, or not present in the provided context.”
    Why does it happen?
      LLM predicts tokens, not facts
      Insufficient or missing information
      Poor RAG retrieval
      Too much or irrelevant context
      High temperature / creative decoding
    How to improve:
      improve retrieval quality, use reranking, provide clear grounding instructions, 
      evaluate the system for faithfulness and correctness.
      In RAG, strong retrieval and grounding are therefore critical to reducing hallucination.”

