17. What is LoRA and QLoRA?
Ans:    अगर हम किसी बड़े LLM को fine-tune करना चाहते हैं, तो पूरे model के billions of parameters को update करना बहुत expensive हो सकता है।
        यहीं LoRA और QLoRA काम आते हैं।
        LoRA = Low-Rank Adaptation: LoRA एक Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) technique है, जिसमें original LLM के weights को freeze करके केवल छोटे trainable matrices को train किया जाता है।
          Original model → Freeze ❄️
             +
          Small LoRA adapters → Train
          इसलिए बहुत कम parameters train करने पड़ते हैं। LoRA सीधे W को change नहीं करता। इसके बजाय एक छोटा update सीखता है: W' = W + ΔW
          जहाँ ΔW को low-rank matrices के रूप में represent किया जाता है।
          इससे: कम GPU memory, कम trainable parameters, कम training cost, faster fine-tuning, original model को preserve करना आसान
        QLoRA: Quantized LoRA ( Quantization + LoRA को combine करता है। )
            QLoRA में base LLM को low-bit precision, typically 4-bit, में quantize करके रखा जाता है और उसके ऊपर LoRA adapters train किए जाते हैं।
            इससे बड़े models को comparatively कम GPU memory में fine-tune करना possible होता है।
            QLoRA = Quantize model + freeze it + train small adapters

18. When would you choose fine-tuning over RAG?
Ans: I would choose fine-tuning over RAG when the problem is primarily about changing the model's behavior, task performance, style, or output format rather than providing external knowledge. 
    For example, classification, consistent structured outputs, or a specialized response style are good fine-tuning use cases. 
    If the requirement is to answer from private, dynamic, or frequently changing knowledge, I would prefer RAG. In many enterprise applications, 
    I may use both—RAG for knowledge grounding and fine-tuning for consistent behavior.”

19. What is model quantization?
Ans: Quantization is a model optimization technique that reduces the precision of model parameters, such as FP16 to INT8 or INT4, to reduce memory and improve inference efficiency with minimal loss in model quality.”
      Quantization = Make the same model lighter
    Why do we quantize?
    Main benefits:
      Lower memory/GPU VRAM
      Smaller model size
      Potentially faster inference
      Lower infrastructure cost
      Allows large LLMs to run on comparatively smaller hardware
20. What is model distillation?
Ans: is a model compression technique where a smaller student model is trained using the outputs or knowledge of a larger teacher model.
      The objective is to retain as much of the teacher's performance as possible while reducing model size, latency, and inference cost.”
      For example=> Teacher: 70B model, Student: 7B model
        The 7B model tries to learn the useful behavior of the 70B model.
        Why do we use distillation?
          Because smaller models can provide:
          Lower inference cost
          Lower latency
          Less memory requirement
          Easier deployment
          Better suitability for edge/on-premise environments

21. How would you select an LLM for an enterprise application?
Ans: I would first define the business use case and evaluation criteria, 
      create a representative enterprise dataset, shortlist multiple models, and run them through the same evaluation framework.
      
      For RAG applications, I would specifically evaluate faithfulness, groundedness, answer relevance, and retrieval quality. 
      I would then assess security, data privacy, compliance, deployment options, latency, throughput, and total cost of ownership. 
      Finally, I would run a production-like POC and use a weighted scorecard to select the model that provides the best overall business value."
      Steps:
      Define the use case: What task will the LLM perform Chatbot, RAG, summarization, coding, classification, extraction, agentic workflow, etc.
      Shortlist suitable models: Compare models based on capabilities such as reasoning, context window, tool calling, structured output, multilingual support, and domain knowledge.
      Evaluate quality using a representative dataset: Measure metrics such as: Accuracy / Correctness, Faithfulness / Groundedness for RAG, Answer relevance, Hallucination rate, Instruction following, Safety / toxicity
      Evaluate performance: Latency, Throughput, Context-window requirements, Concurrency and rate limits.
      Run a Proof of Concept


      
